<!-- Theme — THE app's presentation vocabulary, once. Breakpoints, gutters, page shells,
     the brand palette and the locale-aware formatters, as APP-WIDE FUNCTIONS every surface
     calls instead of re-deriving.

     WHAT THIS REPLACES. Thirteen screens each carried a byte-identical copy of `bp`,
     `wide`, `gutter` and `shell` — AGENTS.md says "responsive is one vocabulary per
     screen", and it was one vocabulary fifteen times. Changing a breakpoint or the
     desktop gutter was a fifteen-file edit an adopter would get half of. Twenty distinct
     brand hexes were written out 529 times across 24 files, so a re-skin was a find-and-
     replace nobody could verify.

     THE MECHANISM is `<functions global="true">` — the framework's GLOBAL FUNCTION
     LIBRARY (OpenSource/Skills/js-core.md "Shared logic", corpus Conformance/functions).
     The block registers into the app-wide JSE table, last write wins, and a surface-local
     name shadows it. Wired on all three renderers, and — measured, which is the part that
     matters — on the SSR path too (`server/src/render.ts` renderInstance, same call as
     the dom layer`s mount.ts).

     ══ THE ONE RULE FOR A CALLER: MOUNT THIS FIRST ═══════════════════════════════════
     Registration happens when this component INSTANTIATES, not at app boot, so the mount
     has to come before the first read. Measured on a cold origin, first request:

       <Theme/> absent            /notices SSR rendered  BP[]        (a silent null)
       <Theme/> as first child    /notices SSR rendered  BP[phone]   (correct)

     A screen root reads its own computeds as soon as it evaluates a `visible-if` on its
     first child — `<TopNav visible-if="dsx.variable.wide"/>` is the usual one — so
     `<Theme/>` goes ABOVE that, as the literal first child of the screen root. It renders
     a 0×0 stack (the DemoSeed idiom) and paints nothing.

     ══ WHY THE PALETTE IS HALF-ROUTED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT ════════════════
     Every colour that a screen writes in MARKUP — an inline `color=`, a `background=`,
     a ternary in a computed, a hole in a `style=` string — calls a function here.

     Every colour inside a `<style as="…">` DECLARATION is still a literal, because a
     `<style>` attribute CANNOT carry an interpolation: the compiler drops the whole
     declaration, silently. Measured 2026-09-01 —

       <style as="a" color="{{ dsx.variable.brand }}" fontSize="15"/>
         → [data-dsx~="a562"] { font-size: 15px; }        (no `color` at all)

     — no error, no lint warning, no CSS. Filed as PLAN.md §6.91. Until it lands, the
     literals inside `<style>` blocks are CHECKED MIRRORS rather than sources: this file
     is the single definition and `npm run check:styles` refuses any hex in Components/**
     that is not one of the named tones below, so a mirror cannot drift silently and a
     re-skin cannot half-land. `npm run review` reads the same table, so the brand-palette
     waiver (§6.16) is derived from this file rather than from a second copy of it.

     Two of the tones below are unreachable from this file today and are named anyway:
     `railShellOf` is called with the 1440 CONTENT cap even on screens whose own shell is
     1100, because the rail is a full-bleed track and the page column is not — that is a
     real difference, not a copy that drifted. -->
<stack width="0" height="0">
  <head>
    <!-- BODY CAVEAT (js-core.md): the NATIVE parsers do not lift a `<functions>` body as
         raw JS yet, so a bare `<` in here breaks them and lint mirrors that by law. Every
         comparison below is therefore spelled with `>=` or `!=`, never `<`, and there is
         no `&&` — an XML body would need it escaped and the escape is easy to lose. -->
    <functions global="true">
      /* ── THE RESPONSIVE VOCABULARY ────────────────────────────────────────────────
         phone 0-767 · tablet 768-1119 · desktop 1120+. The same three names the router
         uses for its motion breakpoint (dsx.config.json router.motionBreakpoint 768) and
         the same the native export puts iPad portrait and iPad Pro landscape on. */
      function bpOf(w) {
        if (w >= 1120) { return 'desktop' }
        if (w >= 768) { return 'tablet' }
        return 'phone'
      }

      /* every screen asked the same question of bp, so it is one name now */
      function isWide(bp) { return bp != 'phone' }

      /* the two-column player collapses at 1120 — ShortMax collapses at 1024 and
         ReelShort keeps a 416 panel; 1120 is this app's measured choice (AGENTS.md) */
      function panelAt(w) { return w >= 1120 }

      /* MEASURED, not chosen: 86 is the category reference gutter on desktop. */
      function gutterOf(bp) {
        if (bp == 'desktop') { return 86 }
        if (bp == 'tablet') { return 40 }
        return 16
      }

      /* the OPERATOR gutter is deliberately tighter — Manage is a dense table surface,
         not a storefront, and 86px of air on a data grid is wasted column */
      function opsGutterOf(bp) {
        if (bp == 'desktop') { return 32 }
        if (bp == 'tablet') { return 24 }
        return 16
      }

      /* THE PAGE SHELL: a centred column capped at maxW with the gutter as its padding.
         maxW is the caller`s, because it is a CONTENT decision — 1440 for a storefront
         grid, 1100 for a purchase column, 760 for an inbox, 520 for a credential form. */
      function shellOf(bp, maxW) {
        const g = gutterOf(bp)
        return 'width: 100%; max-width: ' + maxW + 'px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; padding-left: ' + g + 'px; padding-right: ' + g + 'px'
      }

      function opsShellOf(bp, maxW) {
        const g = opsGutterOf(bp)
        return 'width: 100%; max-width: ' + maxW + 'px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; padding-left: ' + g + 'px; padding-right: ' + g + 'px'
      }

      /* A HORIZONTAL RAIL`S TRACK IS `max-content`, never the page shell`s `width: 100%`
         (AGENTS.md): the shell recipe caps the track at the scroller and the rows eat the
         difference — measured, 150px posters rendered at 72.25px. The lead padding keeps
         the first card flush with the page column on a viewport wider than the cap. */
      function railShellOf(bp, w, maxW) {
        const g = gutterOf(bp)
        const over = w - maxW
        const lead = g + (over > 0 ? Math.round(over / 2) : 0)
        return 'width: max-content; padding-left: ' + lead + 'px; padding-right: ' + lead + 'px'
      }

      /* ── THE BRAND PALETTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Named by JOB, not by hue, so a re-skin is a value change and never a rename.
         Callers: markup only (see the header — a `<style>` cannot hold an interpolation).
         `npm run check:styles` proves no hex in Components/** is outside this table. */

      /* the accent. One job: the thing you tap, the thing that is on. */
      function brand() { return '#FF2C55' }
      function brandTint() { return '#FF6B8A' }      /* accent on a dark card, links */
      function brandPressed() { return '#8E1637' }   /* the followed/pressed state */
      function brandSoft() { return '#FF8FA8' }      /* accent ink on a coloured wash */

      /* money. gold is VIP, coin is a balance — two different promises, two tones. */
      function gold() { return '#F6B63D' }
      function coin() { return '#F7C948' }
      function goldHi() { return '#FFE9A8' }
      function vipGoldTop() { return '#F7E3AE' }
      function vipGoldMid() { return '#E8C77A' }
      function vipGoldEnd() { return '#DDB35E' }
      function onGold() { return '#3A2A08' }         /* ink on a gold card */
      function onGoldSoft() { return '#2A1D04' }

      /* the stage and the ink */
      function stage() { return '#000000' }
      function ink() { return '#FFFFFF' }
      function inkOnLight() { return '#111111' }

      /* the dark surface ramp, deepest first */
      function surfaceDeep() { return '#0A0A0E' }
      function surface() { return '#141419' }
      function surfaceRaised() { return '#17171C' }
      function surfaceCard() { return '#1C1C22' }
      function surfaceInput() { return '#1E1E24' }
      function surfaceEdge() { return '#1F1F26' }
      function surfaceChip() { return '#232329' }
      function surfaceChipOn() { return '#26262C' }
      function surfaceHair() { return '#3A3A42' }
      function surfaceHairLift() { return '#43434D' }

      /* the sheet ramp (the DramaWave drawer set — a sheet sits ABOVE the page, so it
         gets its own neutrals rather than borrowing the page`s) */
      function sheet() { return '#141414' }
      function sheetRaised() { return '#1A1A1C' }
      function sheetCard() { return '#1C1C1E' }
      function sheetChip() { return '#242426' }
      function sheetEdge() { return '#2A2A2E' }
      function sheetCell() { return '#2C2C2E' }

      /* one-off brand moments, each earned */
      function recoveryViolet() { return '#1E1830' }  /* the Stripe settle-recovery card */
      function violetEdge() { return '#3A3547' }
      function positive() { return '#5FD08A' }        /* a grant landed */
      function rankSilver() { return '#B9C5D8' }
      function rankBronze() { return '#CE8B5C' }
      function gemLift() { return '#FF7BAA' }         /* the VIP gem gradient, top stop */
      function gemDeep() { return '#B3103A' }         /* ... and its end stop */

      /* ── THE VIP PROMISE, SAID ONCE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
         Product copy in the presentation library needs a reason, and this is it: a PAID
         PROMISE is the one string an app must never hold two copies of. The benefit list
         and the term sentence are rendered on two surfaces that a viewer can reach within
         one tap of each other — the /vip page and the plans sheet that a nested level now
         opens over — and an adopter who edits one is an adopter who has shipped an app
         that advertises two different products at two different prices.

         Every row below is a thing `server/*.dsx` ACTUALLY GRANTS, and can be checked:
           unlocked  `unlockEpisode` prices at 0 while `vip_until` is in the future
                     (server/wallet.dsx) — no catalogue exceptions, so it covers shows
                     added after the pass was bought
           coins     because that price is 0 the spend path never runs: no ledger row, no
                     wallet debit, so the balance survives the pass
           no ads    <AdGate> takes `vip=` and refuses in its own action
         Three rows and not four. "1080p" and "Offline downloads" were deleted in an
         earlier pass because nothing in this app delivers either; padding the list back
         to four would re-create the defect that removal fixed. */
      function vipBenefits() {
        return [
          { id: 'b1', icon: 'lock.open.fill',         label: 'Every episode unlocked',
            note: 'Paid episodes open at 0 coins, on every series in the catalogue — including the ones added while your pass runs' },
          { id: 'b2', icon: 'dollarsign.circle.fill', label: 'Your coins stay yours',
            note: 'A VIP unlock debits nothing, so the balance you bought is still there when the pass ends' },
          { id: 'b3', icon: 'bolt.fill',              label: 'No ads',
            note: 'The one rewarded ad this app shows is switched off while your pass is active' }
        ]
      }

      /* THE TERM, derived from the tiers the SERVER shipped rather than authored — so the
         day a deployment changes its pass lengths this sentence changes with them. Pass
         `catalog.vip` (or null while it is still loading). */
      function vipTermLine(tiers) {
        const tail = ' When the date passes VIP simply stops — your coins are not affected.'
        if (tiers == null) { return 'Every plan is a one-time charge for a pass that runs a fixed number of days.' + tail }
        if (tiers.length == 0) { return 'Every plan is a one-time charge for a pass that runs a fixed number of days.' + tail }
        let days = ''
        for (const t of tiers) { days = days == '' ? String(t.days) : days + ', ' + String(t.days) }
        return 'Every plan is a one-time charge for a dated pass — ' + days + ' days. Nothing renews and there is nothing to cancel; buying again adds days to the pass you hold.' + tail
      }

      /* ── LOCALE-AWARE FORMATTING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
         Numbers and dates are locale FORMATTING, not translation, and the string table
         cannot help with either: `1.2M` and `2026-09-01` are composed at render time and
         resolve before the lookup. JSE carries the real thing — Intl.NumberFormat,
         Intl.DateTimeFormat and toLocaleDateString are all in the builtin table on every
         renderer — so these are the twins of the four hand-rolled formatters this template
         used to carry (`Math.round(v / 100) / 10 + 'M'` and `String(iso).slice(0, 10)`,
         which print an American number and an ISO date to every reader on earth). */

      /* the ACTIVE locale, or empty for "let the device decide" — the same ladder the
         kernel string seam walks (global.locale, then the device, then en) */
      function activeLocale() {
        const l = global.locale
        if (l == null) { return '' }
        return String(l)
      }

      /* a view count at glance width. The K/M suffixes stay Latin because the compact
         notation Intl would give us is NOT in the framework number formatter (its options
         are style/currency/fraction digits/grouping), so inventing `notation: compact`
         here would work on web and silently print the raw number on a device. What IS
         portable is the NUMERAL, which is what actually differs: es-ES writes 1,2 M. */
      function compactCount(v) {
        if (v == null) { return '' }
        if (v >= 1000000) { return decimalOne(v / 1000000) + 'M' }
        if (v >= 1000) { return decimalOne(v / 1000) + 'K' }
        return String(v)
      }

      function decimalOne(n) {
        const loc = activeLocale()
        const opts = { maximumFractionDigits: 1 }
        if (loc == '') { return new Intl.NumberFormat(undefined, opts).format(n) }
        return new Intl.NumberFormat(loc, opts).format(n)
      }

      /* an ISO timestamp as a date the reader recognises. Slicing the first ten characters
         gave every reader 2026-09-01; this gives 01/09/2026 in es and 9/1/2026 in en-US. */
      function shortDate(iso) {
        if (iso == null) { return '' }
        if (iso == '') { return '' }
        const loc = activeLocale()
        if (loc == '') { return new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString() }
        return new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString(loc)
      }
    </functions>
  </head>
</stack>
